---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/packmetry-workspace.css';
import '../../styles/methodology.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Packing Algorithm & Verification Methodology — Packmetry"
  description="How Packmetry approaches 3D bin packing: deterministic candidate generation, independent verification, objective ranking, canonical utilization metrics, DIM assumptions and the verified plan shown in 3D."
>
  <main class="pm-methodology-article-page">
    <div class="pm-methodology-article-shell">
      <header class="pm-methodology-article-hero">
        <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
          Packing algorithm &amp; verification
        </p>

        <h1>
          From solver candidate
          to verified packing plan.
        </h1>

        <p>
          Packmetry is built around a separation
          of responsibilities: the solver proposes
          arrangements, an independent verifier
          checks them, objective logic ranks only
          verified candidates, and the canonical
          plan becomes the source of truth for
          metrics and 3D presentation.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section
        class="pm-methodology-flow"
        aria-label="Packmetry planning pipeline"
      >
        <div class="pm-methodology-flow-grid">
          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>01</span>
            <h2>Normalize inputs</h2>
            <p>
              Item, carton, unit, constraint and
              objective data enter the shared
              domain model.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>02</span>
            <h2>Generate candidates</h2>
            <p>
              The deterministic baseline solver
              creates a small bounded set of
              heuristic packing candidates.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>03</span>
            <h2>Verify independently</h2>
            <p>
              Every candidate is checked by a
              separate verification boundary before
              it is eligible for recommendation.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>04</span>
            <h2>Rank valid options</h2>
            <p>
              Packing coverage is protected first,
              then the selected objective ranks
              comparable verified candidates.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>05</span>
            <h2>Construct canonical plan</h2>
            <p>
              One selected verified candidate is
              converted into the canonical
              PackingPlan and canonical metrics.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>06</span>
            <h2>Present the result</h2>
            <p>
              Result summaries and the 3D view
              consume the canonical verified plan
              instead of rebuilding placement logic.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="pm-methodology-content">
        <article class="pm-methodology-prose">
          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              What 3D bin packing means here
            </p>

            <h2>
              Packmetry places rectangular item
              instances inside rectangular cartons.
            </h2>

            <p>
              In Packmetry, a 3D packing problem
              asks whether item instances with
              length, width and height can be placed
              inside one or more carton interiors
              without crossing carton boundaries or
              overlapping one another.
            </p>

            <p>
              Placements are axis-aligned. An item
              may use only the orientations allowed
              by its rotation policy. The physical
              packing problem can also be limited by
              known carton inventory and known gross
              weight constraints.
            </p>

            <p>
              A successful geometric arrangement is
              therefore more than a volume check:
              every placed item needs explicit
              coordinates, dimensions and an allowed
              orientation that survive independent
              verification.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Why multiple valid arrangements exist
            </p>

            <h2>
              The same packing problem can have
              more than one valid arrangement.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Different valid plans can result from
              choices such as which carton type is
              opened first, which available
              orientation is accepted, or where a
              valid placement is chosen inside an
              already-open carton.
            </p>

            <p>
              Packmetry does not attempt to enumerate
              every mathematically possible
              arrangement. The current baseline uses
              a small deterministic search policy so
              repeated runs remain reproducible and
              browser cost stays bounded.
            </p>

            <div class="pm-methodology-callout">
              <strong>Current search scope</strong>
              <p>
                The production baseline varies
                carton-type order only. Item order
                and rotation-order diversity are not
                currently explored as separate
                candidate strategies.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Candidate generation
            </p>

            <h2>
              The current baseline is deterministic,
              bounded and heuristic.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Packmetry&apos;s current production
              solver is the deterministic
              <strong>packmetry-baseline</strong>
              solver. It processes item instances
              in input order, tries already-open
              carton instances before opening new
              ones, and uses deterministic
              axis-aligned candidate points and
              allowed rotation order.
            </p>

            <p>
              For the same normalized input, the
              solver evaluates carton types using
              up to three order strategies:
            </p>

            <ol>
              <li>
                carton types in the original input
                order;
              </li>
              <li>
                smallest internal carton volume
                first;
              </li>
              <li>
                largest internal carton volume
                first.
              </li>
            </ol>

            <p>
              Exact duplicate candidate plans are
              removed while preserving the first
              occurrence. The resulting candidate
              set therefore contains between one
              and three candidates.
            </p>

            <div class="pm-methodology-callout">
              <strong>Important limitation</strong>
              <p>
                This is a bounded heuristic search,
                not exhaustive 3D bin-packing
                enumeration. Packmetry describes
                the result as a recommended or best
                plan found, not a guaranteed global
                mathematical optimum.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Independent verification
            </p>

            <h2>
              Solver output is treated as an
              untrusted claim.
            </h2>

            <p>
              A candidate does not become trusted
              merely because Packmetry generated it.
              Each candidate passes through a
              separate verifier before ranking or
              canonical-plan construction.
            </p>

            <p>
              The current verifier checks, among
              other invariants:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>
                referenced item and carton IDs
                exist;
              </li>
              <li>
                item instance indexes are valid and
                item instances are counted once;
              </li>
              <li>
                placement dimensions match the
                declared rotation and rotation
                policy;
              </li>
              <li>
                placements stay inside carton
                internal boundaries;
              </li>
              <li>
                placements do not overlap;
              </li>
              <li>
                known gross-weight limits are not
                exceeded;
              </li>
              <li>
                carton inventory limits are not
                overused.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              Verification failures remain
              observable. The planning pipeline
              does not silently repair an invalid
              candidate and present the repaired
              version as if the solver had produced
              a valid result.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Recommendation logic
            </p>

            <h2>
              Coverage comes before optimization.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Only independently verified
              candidates are eligible for ranking.
              Before an optimization objective is
              applied, Packmetry first maximizes
              placed item instances and then
              minimizes unplaced item instances.
            </p>

            <p>
              This prevents an objective such as
              fewer cartons from winning simply
              because an incomplete candidate used
              fewer boxes. Only candidates tied at
              the best packing-coverage level move
              on to objective-specific comparison.
            </p>

            <p>
              Current product objectives can then
              compare verified candidates using
              deterministic lexicographic metrics
              such as carton count, wasted internal
              volume, utilization, known carton
              cost, known gross-weight data or
              dimensional weight where the required
              data is available.
            </p>

            <p>
              Packmetry does not collapse different
              units such as cartons, cubic
              millimetres, currency and grams into
              one arbitrary weighted score in the
              current baseline selection policy.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Utilization
            </p>

            <h2>
              Utilization is derived from verified
              placed volume and internal carton
              volume.
            </h2>

            <p>
              For each used carton, Packmetry sums
              the volume of its verified placements:
              <strong>
                item volume = Σ(length × width ×
                height).
              </strong>
              Carton volume is calculated from the
              carton&apos;s internal length, width
              and height.
            </p>

            <p>
              Empty volume is:
              <strong>
                carton volume - item volume.
              </strong>
              Carton utilization is:
              <strong>
                item volume / carton volume.
              </strong>
            </p>

            <p>
              At plan level, the same quantities are
              summed across all used cartons before
              overall utilization is calculated.
              If a plan uses zero cartons, overall
              utilization is defined as zero.
            </p>

            <div class="pm-methodology-callout">
              <strong>What utilization does not prove</strong>
              <p>
                A favorable volume ratio cannot
                prove that items geometrically fit.
                Utilization is a metric derived
                after verified placement; it is not
                a replacement for 3D geometry.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Canonical result
            </p>

            <h2>
              The selected verified candidate is
              converted into one canonical plan.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Canonical plan construction derives
              final carton contents, placements,
              status and metrics from the selected
              verified candidate. Canonical status
              is derived from verified item
              accounting rather than blindly copied
              from a solver status field.
            </p>

            <p>
              Utilization and empty-volume metrics
              use carton internal dimensions,
              because those dimensions describe the
              usable packing cavity. Known physical
              weight metrics remain separate and
              unknown values are not silently
              treated as zero.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              3D presentation
            </p>

            <h2>
              The visualization follows the
              canonical plan.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Packmetry&apos;s 3D view is a
              comprehension layer, not another
              packing engine. It receives canonical
              verified placements and renders those
              coordinates. It does not independently
              invent a more attractive arrangement.
            </p>

            <p>
              This keeps the textual result,
              metrics and 3D arrangement attached
              to the same source of truth.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Dimensional weight
            </p>

            <h2>
              DIM weight uses external package size
              and an explicit unit-aware divisor.
            </h2>

            <p>
              When dimensional-weight analysis is
              enabled for a carton, Packmetry uses
              external package dimensions rather
              than the internal packing cavity.
              Length, width and height are converted
              into the divisor&apos;s length unit,
              multiplied into package volume, and
              divided by the explicit divisor.
            </p>

            <p>
              The result is interpreted in the
              divisor&apos;s mass unit and converted
              to canonical grams. The core DIM
              calculation does not apply
              carrier-billing rounding.
            </p>

            <p>
              When both actual gross weight and DIM
              weight are known, the base estimated
              chargeable weight is the greater of
              the two. Missing actual weight or
              missing DIM data remains unknown
              rather than being replaced with zero.
            </p>

            <p>
              DIM settings can participate in
              verified-candidate ranking and
              canonical metrics, but the baseline
              solver does not move items differently
              because a DIM divisor was supplied.
              Packmetry also does not turn that DIM
              result into live carrier pricing,
              service-specific billing rules, zones
              or shipping-rate quotes.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Assumptions &amp; current limits
            </p>

            <h2>
              Packmetry keeps unknowns and
              unsupported constraints explicit.
            </h2>

            <ul>
              <li>
                canonical packing dimensions are
                represented in millimetres;
              </li>
              <li>
                canonical mass is represented in
                grams;
              </li>
              <li>
                internal carton dimensions govern
                packing geometry and utilization;
              </li>
              <li>
                external carton dimensions govern
                carton DIM calculations;
              </li>
              <li>
                unknown item or carton tare weight
                is not silently treated as zero;
              </li>
              <li>
                deterministic ordering is part of
                the current baseline behavior;
              </li>
              <li>
                the current baseline does not
                perform exhaustive search.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="pm-methodology-callout">
              <strong>Current constraint boundary</strong>
              <p>
                Rotation policy, boundaries,
                overlap, known gross-weight limits
                and inventory bounds are enforced by
                the current solver/verifier path.
                Fragile handling, padding, spacing
                and stackability remain outside the
                currently enforced semantics where
                Packmetry cannot yet guarantee those
                behaviors.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>
        </article>

        <aside class="pm-methodology-aside">
          <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
            Current baseline
          </p>

          <strong>
            What Packmetry can truthfully claim
          </strong>

          <ul>
            <li>
              axis-aligned 3D carton packing with
              supported rotation policies;
            </li>
            <li>
              deterministic bounded candidate
              generation;
            </li>
            <li>
              independent verification before
              recommendation;
            </li>
            <li>
              coverage-first, objective-aware
              ranking of verified candidates;
            </li>
            <li>
              canonical utilization, volume and
              known-weight metrics;
            </li>
            <li>
              DIM calculations with explicit unit
              semantics when required data exists;
            </li>
            <li>
              3D rendering from canonical verified
              placements;
            </li>
            <li>
              no claim of guaranteed global
              optimality.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="pm-methodology-links">
            <a href="/personal/">
              Try the Personal workspace →
            </a>

            <a href="/business/">
              Open the Business workspace →
            </a>

            <a href="/tools/dimensional-weight-calculator/">
              Use the DIM calculator →
            </a>

            <a href="/guides/dimensional-weight/">
              Read the DIM guide →
            </a>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <section class="pm-methodology-cta">
        <div>
          <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
            Put the methodology to work
          </p>

          <h2>
            Run a real packing problem through
            the same verified pipeline.
          </h2>

          <p>
            Personal keeps the workflow simple.
            Business exposes more operational data
            such as carton inventory, costs,
            weights and DIM settings while using
            the same core planning architecture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pm-methodology-cta-actions">
          <a href="/personal/">
            Personal workspace
          </a>

          <a
            class="pm-methodology-secondary-link"
            href="/business/"
          >
            Business workspace
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
